[{"id":"VRCE-2-1-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":1,"english":"Then the ascetic asked Bharata, the son of Kaikeyi, when he had decided for staying there, to receive his hospitality.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.143,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-91-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f17c4f1981d283e77aa441b61ec3200ee17f20ed3cfa04fcda86a7817decd735","compact":"4a95696e1f2bdcd872219eb7053a434ab25688407f8f1bf9d04d3a7a3dfbf02e","nasal":"d5b99baa54d8dd3ba09f74f5ef999c5c67590d6d81855cc3e3151ab1fbf7b2b1"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":2,"english":"And is the revered one will, and your maternal uncle, Yuddhajit? And, my son, have you passed your time pleasantly during your sojourn? It behoves you to tell me all this.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.179,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-72-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bd736efd28b4a24b9815cce75ce0ee01fdb952f39054531e977e9f6450cb1d26","compact":"13a882620aeaeb40d6a839c6c2543ebd540a360b9f26dd4d4e4a777fbce3235c","nasal":"13a882620aeaeb40d6a839c6c2543ebd540a360b9f26dd4d4e4a777fbce3235c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":3,"english":"Having said this to Sumitrā, that one emaciated and with a pallid countenance, trembling and almost deprived of sensation, went to where Bharata, was. And it came to pass that the king's son, Bharata, along with Śatrughna had just then by the way that led to the same, been proceeding to Kausalyā's quarter.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.179,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-75-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a272995f549e8356444f6d33f63303e3c1116ff5acaeb484115a24acf1cb1c44","compact":"32465e39508b0bc07644d21bff9a32ab239703fe1c1323c614e147f2d0bcd8d3","nasal":"32465e39508b0bc07644d21bff9a32ab239703fe1c1323c614e147f2d0bcd8d3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":4,"english":"Then after speaking with his maternal grandfather and uncle, Yudhajit, Bharata ascending a car, set out with Satrughna.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.134,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-70-28"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e3307838f52a10c2a9d861113ecf7e530bad2792ef289d483ceb3ed4a70ccb85","compact":"521bfff73f96c43370597d0518aee37a04d2f9dcd9b4cd61a9f82b4a18e4f70e","nasal":"bb3b13cba1098f763634432254b0e37690ee44ae1607a10804ab8833e903d866"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":5,"english":"He was aye of quiescent soul; and always precluded his speech with an amiable phrase; and although he might be addressed in a harsh manner, yet he returned no corresponding reply.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.448,"method":"fuzzy-nearby-witness","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-1-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a3c3a884422cdfabf457bcd0fff6b78fd63a2cc412180474f50d8fac1394a0c4","compact":"cc0e47f32f274aa92609c4c5a74648b79796ba595dc0c0b0af6c1738540c1fb0","nasal":"cc0e47f32f274aa92609c4c5a74648b79796ba595dc0c0b0af6c1738540c1fb0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":6,"english":"And there he abode with his brother, being ministered to in very respect and tended by his maternal uncle, Aśvapati, with all the fondness of a father (with the fondness shown to a son).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-1-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d7e0be1de140159aa71ba327983a1f5f457fe5215b51bd0a5252d31b7995f523","compact":"3847244852bd3a8e1578c9b0e16e64a9d246c543f0ee6c5cd8eba463521c2ef6","nasal":"3847244852bd3a8e1578c9b0e16e64a9d246c543f0ee6c5cd8eba463521c2ef6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":7,"english":"Albeit thus staying, with every ministration extended towards them as much as they could wish, yet those heroic brothers failed not to remember the aged king Daſaratha.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-1-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"40fb93817d8ca9b3e22448d128501179b2294c0c577d31b30bf54a5bf5aa1121","compact":"47ecc3a4410e2a5eb1f5bc8a784950d26147b6455ad44b3bae280df35ab60948","nasal":"4556c4643ea56d63b31e5fc6157dd8b0f521996a586ae2fd8056aa7c3225fbc2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":8,"english":"And the puissant king also on his pare remembered his sons away from home, Bharata and Śatrughna, resembling the mighty Indra and Varuna.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-1-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ff0361a343f763157f5d9fc6de891ceefe2421f529c214927e651a90d1d4497a","compact":"bbef950f066c044f74b9a2f60a267d6ff918d74129c2ba2f7f84525f526095db","nasal":"3c01d2905e5fd327895f44c40751e6e53f15c853a894d8415a1b056c7e0fd125"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":9,"english":"All those four chiefs of men were dear to him even as four hands issuing from his own body.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-1-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1a6951cd33a2877e403559ddca78670e8ac6b7c2d7b326499cff4d447bb39589","compact":"af9154cdde43d0f7fdc2bbc9fcb621aca704ca898f2ead40028af25a573d6aa8","nasal":"af9154cdde43d0f7fdc2bbc9fcb621aca704ca898f2ead40028af25a573d6aa8"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":10,"english":"Yet among them all, that highly energetic. Rāma was the favourite of his sire. He was the foremost of in every virtue, like to Svayambhu's (Brahmā) self in the esteem of creation.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-1-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2cc4a54f2bea804f306f5251086163fc1c2094aa3a232916b110718cc2fd3dd2","compact":"fc3ea4100b07065a814e26f5c2976e5cb8e7d71150c2ad43d5615566b1923fa6","nasal":"2c5f3b17dd972d68ab350096c34c0d10cce889532da4666cf183e9da4ed19f8f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-1-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":1,"verse":11,"english":"Rāma and that mighty car-warrior Lakşmaņa swiftly neared the king stricken with grief and rendered senseless by sorrow.","alignment":{"type":"approximate","confidence":0.12,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-34-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2001011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9b5b76df30372c30834151469519813215fba97de491a0cb67dfd35919e0b776","compact":"e849eef2f76d574ae1f75bbf5f45023a29457d2bf348b2b46a432f8a747b6dc4","nasal":"e849eef2